design_rationale:
WORKLOG / DOSSIER — task 1 (seller_standing_reviews_account_actions), revision 4 (policy-task audit revision_2 reworked)

[Routing decision] As in task 0: the blueprint is frozen, and every computed value is a whole number of violation points booked on a standing_events points-expiry entry. Every write lowers standing_records.violation_points by exactly its points, so the field stays the running sum of the ledger. Reinstatement is excluded because of its payout trigger. The seller's own act is the voluntary closure, whose number is the points retired at closing, booked as a retirement entry (tool close_selling_account_c808e333). The rest stays on the closed record as its active points, which a returning seller's new record opens with (declared overwrite of standing_agg_multi_holding A2).

[Step 0 draw] draw_counts.py --task 1 -> prerequisite_problems 3, factor_counts [4, 5, 6, 4]: listing expiry 4, serious step-down 4, closure 5, top-performer relief 6.

[Scope] Task 0 occupies clean-span relief and action-plan relief. Claimed here:
- standing_ext_listing_violation_expiry (book_listing_violation_expiry_caba69b5, agent);
- standing_ext_serious_violation_step_down (book_serious_step_down_71a20607, agent);
- standing_ext_top_performer_relief (grant_top_performer_relief_7abd26c5, agent);
- standing_ext_closure_carry_forward (close_selling_account_c808e333, user).
Clean-span relief is out of scope for a Top Rated seller, and action-plan relief is not requested.

[Audit revision_2 — responses by item id (1.md and _difficulty.md); round-1 closures kept]
- F-5 (suspension/appeal ladders penalised an error-overturned suspension): closed by replacement. Every factor that read reinstated_date or appeals is gone (the full list is in task 0's dossier). This task's replacements:
  - top_performer_severity_credit: no serious points 1 / serious points only from violations six or more months old 0.5 / a serious violation in the last six months 0;
  - top_performer_remediation_credit: FBN disposal in six months 1 / return-to-seller only 0.5 / none 0;
  - at_risk_hold: at_risk with active points at or above the warning threshold 2 / at_risk below it 1 / good_standing 0.
  These read violation severity and age, remediation evidence and account-health status, which a seller-performance program weighs. No rule now reads suspension or appeal history. The suspension episode is no longer seeded: the record has never been suspended (reinstated_date empty, no appeal rows, no reversal), and the persona says 'never' if asked. The scored values are unchanged: top-performer relief 4 (severity 0.5 + remediation 0.5 replace history 0.5 + appeal 0.5), closure 2 (at-risk hold 1 replaces suspension hold 1).
- O-5 (step-down compute does not exclude a stepped-down violation; no second-period writer): narrowed further. The rule states the floor at zero and 'steps down once'. A second step-down or a second-period expiry needs per-violation entry dating that the computation language cannot express, and the frozen schema gives entries no reason field. In this world the step-down is booked once, and the intellectual_property violation reaches its second period on 2027-03-04, after the handling date. Observation, no consequence.
- O-3: answered in task 0's dossier; it does not arise here (clean-span relief is out of scope for this seller).
- O-13 (appeal-credit gap): closed; the appeal credit is removed.
- O-14 (contract change under the same uuid8): closed. The closure tool has a fresh name, close_selling_account_c808e333, in task_tools.py, the charter, the gold calls and the charter scope.
- O-15 (four-decimal guard): closed. The top-performer, step-down and closure rules state "products are worked to four decimal places before any rounding down".
- O-16 (retiring points on voluntary closure versus carry-forward): kept as designed. The closure retires points tied to listings, which end with the account. Serious points recorded in the last twelve months count at half, so most of a recent serious violation is kept, and the holds keep more back for recent listing violations and for a seller closing while at risk. Retirement is capped at 1-3 points by tenure. What is kept carries to any returning record, so the anti-evasion purpose holds.
- O-17: task 0 only (persona fixed there).
- Round-1 closures kept, as the audit confirmed: F-1, F-2, F-3, F-4, F-T1-1, F-T1-2, F-T1-3. All four edges still bear weight, the listing expiry and the step-down still commute, every policy keeps exactly one honest method factor, and the cross-difficulty reads are still the FBN removal evidence only.
- F-T1-1 under the new seed: the top-performer near-misses are the restricted_product violation exactly six whole months back (band window) and the disposal fee seven whole months back (remediation credit; counting it gives the disposal credit 1 -> 4.9 -> 4, the same figure, but it flips the step-down to 'all carried points' -> 4). The severity credit's six-month line is crossed by the intellectual_property violation at seven months.

[Why a good test] The seller asks to close his account. The closure rule fixes the retired points only after any top-performer relief owed on the evaluation day stands booked. Today is the evaluation day, he is Top Rated, and that relief is owed. The relief rule is not worked out until the listing-type expiry and the serious step-down that have fallen due are booked, and the activity shows both missing. Four writes follow in topological order:
1. listing expiry 2 (step 8);
2. step-down 3 (step 10; steps 1 and 2 may go in either order);
3. relief 4 (step 12);
4. hand over the closure retiring 2 (step 14).
Every computation combines tiered parameters with a shape-changing method on handling-time worlds. Three read the seller's FBN removal records in another difficulty: stock-hold offset, step-down stock rule, remediation credit.

[Problem-tree card] Root closure (user) <- top-performer relief (agent) <- {listing expiry, serious step-down} (agents).
- Closure <- relief: value_input, gate cf_ser_active, state form, affirmative: "The retired points are fixed only after any top-performer relief owed on the evaluation day stands booked on the standing record."
- Relief <- listing expiry and relief <- step-down: value_input, gate tp_active, state form, negated: "Top-performer relief is not worked out until every listing-type violation whose listing expiry period has run carries its expiry entry." / "... every serious violation whose first period has run carries its step-down entry."
- Four-point test holds for each sentence. Each state is produced by exactly one write of this difficulty.
- False at the opening: listing_policy violation 2026-02-09 has no entry; intellectual_property violation 2026-03-04 has no entry; evaluation recorded today with no relief entry.
- Deletion runs: skip expiry -> relief 6; skip step-down -> relief 6; skip relief -> retired 3.
- The persona names no prerequisite, and the tools check none.

[Base-invariant card] The three points-expiry writes and the closure's retirement entry are governed by standing_violation_rules. Their gates are replaced by declared overwrites, so each declares policy_scope. The closure is also governed by standing_closure: the record is at_risk, an allowed status. Time conditions count whole months back from the handling date:
- listing_policy violation at MONTHS 8 against period 8 -> run (at 7 it would not be);
- intellectual_property violation at MONTHS 7 against 6 -> run;
- the evaluation is recorded today (the 20th).

[Business-object card] One record, three violations: listing_policy 2, intellectual_property 8, restricted_product 4. Entry targets:
- expiry -> listing_policy violation;
- step-down -> intellectual_property violation (8 -> 5);
- relief -> the violation carrying the most points: intellectual_property 5 against restricted_product 4 (5 -> 1);
- retirement -> the listing-type violation carrying the most points: restricted_product 4 (4 -> 2).
Every entry carries its violation's type.

[Factor cards]
Listing expiry (expected 2, all-baseline 6):
- start period 4 (baseline 6 -> 0);
- extension 2 (-> 6);
- count basis, all types counted (-> 6);
- stock-hold offset 2 (-> 6). Near-miss: the disposal of 2026-03-10 is seven months back.
Step-down (expected 3, all-baseline 1):
- share 0.2 (-> 2);
- quiet 1.5 (-> 2);
- stock rule 'share plus a quarter' (-> 2). Near-miss: counting the old disposal gives 'all carried points' 8, capped 4.
- tenure cap 4 (-> 2).
Top-performer relief (expected 4, all-baseline 1):
- base rate 0.2 (-> 3);
- repeat +0.1 (-> 3);
- band 'excess in full' (-> 3). Near-miss: the 2026-04-14 violation is six months back, outside the window.
- severity credit 0.5 (-> 3);
- remediation credit 0.5 (-> 3);
- tenure cap 6 (-> 2).
Closure (expected 2, all-baseline 1):
- retirement share 1.0 (-> 0);
- basis 'listing plus half serious' (-> 3);
- recent-listing hold 1 (-> 3). Near-miss: the restricted_product violation is exactly six months back; counting it as inside holds 2 -> 1.
- at-risk hold 1 (-> 3);
- tenure cap 3 (-> 1).
Multi-source: listing_expiry_base_period, stock_hold_offset, serious_step_share, top_performer_rate, retirement_share, at_risk_hold. Cross-difficulty: stock_hold_offset, serious_remediation_scope, top_performer_remediation_credit (fulfill).

[Seed design] Seller profile opened 2022-11-07, never suspended. Standing violations:
- listing_policy 2 on 2026-02-09;
- intellectual_property 8 on 2026-03-04 (at_risk from that day, points 2 -> 10);
- restricted_product 4 on 2026-04-14 (points 14 < 16).
Ledger 14 recorded, nothing removed = violation_points 14. Today's evaluation: Top Rated Level. FBN: Premier Program, active, 164 units; a completed disposal of 24 units (24 x 0.42 = 10.08) on 2026-03-10 and a completed return of 40 units (40 x 1.30 = 52.00) on 2026-06-15, each with its removal order. No payments, selling or appeal rows: no computation reads them. Every seeded row is read by a gold lookup.

[Persona] Bartholomew opens with the closure request and his retirement, and gives phone and tax last four when asked (email spare). He answers the must-ask questions: why he is closing, that he understands closing ends selling, never suspended. He confirms the closure with exactly what is read out, asks once what retired or kept points mean, and closes the call.

[Standard solution and recomputation] Reads: find_customers -> verify(phone, tax last4) -> find_seller_standing -> find_seller_standing_activity -> find_fbn_enrollments -> find_fbn_activity -> find_fbn_removal_orders. Writes:
1. listing expiry (listing_policy violation, 2);
2. step-down (intellectual_property violation, 3);
3. top-performer relief (intellectual_property violation, 4);
4. hand over the closure (restricted_product violation, 2 retired; the closed record keeps 3 = 14 - 2 - 3 - 4 - 2).
Recomputed with the gate's evaluator on each handling-time world (figures as in the cards and edge runs). A draft from the published rules, schemas, finder returns and persona alone gives the same tree and figures.


[KB audit (policy_kb revision_1) — responses by note; no change to standing.extended.yaml or task_tools.py]
- Note 4 (false figure in the closure edge's load-bearing narrative): corrected. With the top-performer relief skipped the record carries 9 active points, so the at-risk hold is 2, not 1: 6 - 1 - 2 = 3, within the cap 3. The stated skipped value (3) was right and is unchanged. Every other proof figure in this file was re-swept against the current seed and recomputed with the gate evaluator; no other stale or false figure was found:
  - the other two load-bearing runs: relief 6 at 11 and 12 active points;
  - the flips_to_true_by and why_false lines;
  - business effects and call objectives;
  - all factor traces and sensitivities;
  - the points tracings;
  - the seed-consistency checks;
  - the dossier cards.
- Note 1 (point_expiry_days, 180 calendar days, survives in the base parameters): no change. The parameter belongs to the frozen base charter (parameter_schema and every variant) and cannot be removed from the extended copy, whose base section must stay verbatim. The clause that applied it is retired by declared overwrites in every extended policy, and the KB states that clause as no longer in force. The runtime answer stays unique (the audit confirms the visible surface excludes the 180-day reading).
- Note 2 (the repeat adjustment is defined against 'the base rate'): no change; this is the source's intended structure. The top-performer rate is defined in the rule as the base rate plus the repeat adjustment, so the factor document has to name the base-rate quantity it adds to. It names a quantity, not a document or another factor's tier.
- Note 3 (split of an aggregated expiry or step-down amount when several violations qualify): no change.
  - The listing expiry and step-down rules book one entry per violation, each carrying that violation's reference and type, and work the points out per listing type and per serious type.
  - In this world exactly one listing_policy violation (2026-02-09) has run its period and exactly one serious violation (2026-03-04) has run its first period, so the per-type amount is that violation's entry.
  - Pinning a multi-violation split would need a charter change. The coordinator asked to keep the charter unchanged after the KB audit, and no world in this difficulty reaches the case.
- Observation (hypothetical figures in the closure computation and factor documents coincide with this record before the relief): no change. They follow from the fixed tariff (2 / 4 / 8). The documents label them hypothetical, carry no dates or references and state no order between problems.
- Observation ('basis' in the retirement-share worked line): no change. It names the quantity the share multiplies, not a document, tier or variation.
